\begin{abstract}
We study the \emph{free-witness family} of a modulus $N$: the invariants
$R_k(N) = \#\{x \in (\Z/N)^\times : x^k = 1\}$, indexed by an exponent $k$. These are the canonical
structural quantities extractable from $N$ alone, without side information, and they underlie a
large class of classical factoring heuristics.

We prove a complete classification of the family and derive a sharp map of what it can and cannot
do. The \emph{trace lemma} states that $R_k(pq) = \gcd(k,p-1)\gcd(k,q-1)$ for a semiprime,
$R_k(N) = \prod_{r \mid N}\gcd(k,r-1)$ for a squarefree modulus, and
$R_k(N) = \prod_{p \mid N} \gcd(\varphi(p^{v_p(N)}), k)$ for every odd modulus. Three consequences
follow. \emph{(i)} For every finite exponent set $S$ and every prime $q$ there are infinitely many
primes $p$ whose semiprimes $pq$ share one and the same joint profile $(R_k(pq))_{k \in S}$; hence no
function of the joint profile returns a prime factor, and the obstruction is information-theoretic
rather than computational. \emph{(ii)} The family is nonetheless complete in the limit:
$R_k(N) = \varphi(N)$ holds exactly for the multiples of the Carmichael exponent $\lambda(N)$, and a
single complete witness yields the factorisation in closed form; the least positive complete exponent
is exactly $\lcm(p-1,q-1) = \varphi(N)/\gcd(p-1,q-1)$ for a semiprime and
$\lcm_{p \mid N}\varphi(p^{v_p(N)})$ for every odd $N$ --- exponential in $\log N$. \emph{(iii)} Order
finding computes a \emph{classified} coordinate of this same family: for $N=pq$ with distinct odd
primes there are exactly four square roots of unity, and each of the two nontrivial ones splits $N$
by a single greatest common divisor. The quantum speedup therefore bypasses the aggregation cost, not
the classification.

We further prove that the multiplicative smooth-step walk $x \mapsto xs \bmod N$ is sterile --- all
visited values are units, so its gcd channel is identically trivial and its orbit is a coset of
$\langle s \rangle$ --- and we delimit the framework's scope by pricing an external hint: the additive
hint $p+q$ amplifies to the full factorisation in closed form and determines the ordered factor pair
uniquely.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} integer factorisation, roots of unity, Carmichael function, Dirichlet's
theorem, Chinese remainder theorem, order finding, aggregation barrier, cryptographic hardness.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The problem and the frame}

Let $N$ be a composite integer of unknown factorisation. The best classical algorithms --- sieve
methods above all --- run in subexponential but superpolynomial time, and no proof of classical
hardness is known. A productive intermediate goal is \emph{channel-restricted} impossibility: fix a
natural class of quantities an algorithm may extract from $N$, and show the class carries too little
information. The class studied here is the canonical one attached to the multiplicative structure of
$\Z/N$.

\begin{definition}[Free witness]
For integers $N \ge 1$ and $k \ge 0$, the \emph{free witness of exponent $k$} is
\[
  R_k(N) \;=\; \#\{x \in (\Z/N\Z)^\times : x^k = 1\},
\]
the number of $k$-th roots of unity in the unit group modulo $N$.
\end{definition}

The name records two properties: the quantity depends on $N$ alone (it is \emph{free} of side
information), and its value constrains the factorisation. Pollard's $p-1$ method, order finding, and
their relatives all read from this family.

\subsection{Summary of the main statements}

\[
\begin{array}{ll}
\textbf{Trace lemma} & R_k(pq) = \gcd(k,p-1)\gcd(k,q-1) \\[2pt]
\textbf{Information budget} & R_k(pq) \mid k^2, \qquad R_k(r_1\cdots r_n) \le k^n \\[2pt]
\textbf{Joint closure} & \forall\, S \text{ finite},\ \nexists\, F:\ F\big((R_k(pq))_{k\in S}\big)=p \\[2pt]
\textbf{Completeness criterion} & R_k(pq)=\varphi(N) \iff \lcm(p-1,q-1)\mid k \\[2pt]
\textbf{Aggregation depth} & \min\{k>0: R_k(N)=\varphi(N)\} = \lambda(N) \\[2pt]
\textbf{Residue sufficiency} & \exists\, a:\ a^2\equiv 1,\ a\not\equiv\pm1 \Rightarrow \gcd(a-1,N)\in\{p,q\} \\[2pt]
\textbf{Hint amplification} & p = \tfrac12\big(s-\sqrt{s^2-4N}\big),\quad s = p+q
\end{array}
\]

\section{Preliminaries}

We write $\varphi$ for Euler's totient, $\lambda$ for the Carmichael function, $\omega(N)$ for the
number of distinct prime factors and $v_p(N)$ for the $p$-adic valuation. Three classical inputs are
used, and nothing deeper.

\begin{fact}[Cyclic root count]\label{fact:cyc}
In a finite cyclic group of order $n$, the equation $x^k = 1$ has exactly $\gcd(n,k)$ solutions.
\end{fact}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
With $G = \langle g\rangle$, $g^{jk}=1$ iff $n \mid jk$ iff $\tfrac{n}{\gcd(n,k)} \mid j$, and exactly
$\gcd(n,k)$ residues $j$ modulo $n$ satisfy this. Equivalently, the solution set is the kernel of the
$k$-th power endomorphism, whose image has index $\gcd(n,k)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{fact}[Chinese remainder theorem for units]\label{fact:crt}
If $\gcd(m,n)=1$ then $(\Z/mn)^\times \cong (\Z/m)^\times \times (\Z/n)^\times$ as groups.
\end{fact}

\begin{fact}[Dirichlet]\label{fact:dir}
For $\gcd(a,M)=1$, $M \ge 1$, the progression $a, a+M, a+2M,\dots$ contains infinitely many primes.
\end{fact}

\begin{lemma}[Isomorphism invariance and multiplicativity]\label{lem:rho}
Write $\rho_k(G) = \#\{x \in G: x^k=1\}$. Then $\rho_k$ is invariant under group isomorphism and
$\rho_k(G\times H) = \rho_k(G)\rho_k(H)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
An isomorphism $e$ carries solutions to solutions in both directions since $e(x)^k = e(x^k)$. For the
product, $(x,y)^k = (x^k,y^k)$, so the solution set is a product of solution sets.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Multiplicativity of the free witness]\label{lem:mult}
If $\gcd(m,n)=1$ then $R_k(mn) = R_k(m)R_k(n)$, and $R_k(1)=1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Combine Facts~\ref{fact:crt} and Lemma~\ref{lem:rho}; the trivial modulus has trivial unit group.
\end{proof}

Lemma~\ref{lem:mult} is the precise sense in which the family is \emph{CRT-separable}: it never mixes
information across coprime factors. This makes the classification tractable and the family weak.

\section{The trace lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Trace lemma]\label{thm:trace}
Let $p \ne q$ be primes and $N = pq$. Then for all $k \ge 0$,
\[
  R_k(N) \;=\; \gcd(p-1,k)\cdot\gcd(q-1,k).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Fact~\ref{fact:crt}, $(\Z/N)^\times \cong (\Z/p)^\times \times (\Z/q)^\times$. By
Lemma~\ref{lem:rho} the root count is invariant under this isomorphism and multiplicative across the
product. Each factor is cyclic of order $p-1$, respectively $q-1$, so Fact~\ref{fact:cyc} gives
$\gcd(p-1,k)$ and $\gcd(q-1,k)$ solutions.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Symmetry]
$R_k(pq) = R_k(qp)$: the family is symmetric in the factors and can never distinguish $p$ from $q$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Information budget]\label{cor:budget}
$R_k(pq) \mid k^2$; hence a single free witness carries at most $2\log_2 k$ bits, uniformly in $N$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Square roots of unity]\label{cor:four}
If $p \ne q$ are distinct \emph{odd} primes then $R_2(pq) = 4$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Squarefree trace lemma]\label{thm:sqfree}
Let $P$ be a finite set of distinct primes and $N = \prod_{r\in P} r$. Then
$R_k(N) = \prod_{r \in P}\gcd(r-1,k)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induct on $|P|$ with Lemma~\ref{lem:mult}, base case $R_k(1)=1$. At each step the new prime is coprime
to the product of the others, and its local factor is $\gcd(r-1,k)$ by Fact~\ref{fact:cyc}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The residue coordinate counts prime factors]\label{cor:count}
For odd squarefree $N$ with $\omega(N)=n$, $R_2(N) = 2^n$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}
For $k \ge 1$, $R_k(N) \le k^{\omega(N)}$ for squarefree $N$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Trace lemma for odd moduli]\label{thm:odd}
Let $N$ be odd and nonzero. Then
\[
  R_k(N) \;=\; \prod_{p \mid N} \gcd\!\big(\varphi(p^{v_p(N)}),\, k\big).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Decompose $N$ into pairwise coprime prime powers and apply Lemma~\ref{lem:mult} inductively over the
set of prime divisors. Each local unit group $(\Z/p^{v_p})^\times$ is cyclic of order
$\varphi(p^{v_p})$ because $p$ is odd, so Fact~\ref{fact:cyc} gives the local gcd.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[The 2-adic obstruction]
Oddness is used exactly once, and essentially: for $e \ge 3$ the group
$(\Z/2^e)^\times \cong \Z/2 \times \Z/2^{e-2}$ is not cyclic, so its root count is
$\gcd(2,k)\gcd(2^{e-2},k)$ rather than $\gcd(2^{e-1},k)$. The even part therefore \emph{shortens} the
aggregation depth by a bounded factor.
\end{remark}

\section{Joint closure: aggregation is information-theoretically insufficient}

\begin{definition}[Joint profile]
For a finite $S \subseteq \Z_{>0}$, the \emph{$S$-profile} of $N$ is
$\Prof_S(N): k \mapsto R_k(N)$ for $k \in S$ and $0$ otherwise, so that profiles of different moduli
are comparable as objects of one type.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Saturating prime]
A prime $p$ \emph{saturates} $S$ if $\gcd(p-1,k)=k$ for all $k \in S$, i.e.\ if $k \mid p-1$ for all
$k \in S$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Saturating primes are abundant]\label{lem:sat}
Let $S$ be finite with $M = \prod_{k\in S} k$. For every bound $n$ there is a prime $p > n$ with
$p \equiv 1 \pmod M$, and every such prime saturates $S$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Fact~\ref{fact:dir} supplies infinitely many primes $p \equiv 1 \pmod M$, in particular one exceeding
$n$. Then $M \mid p-1$ and $k \mid M$ for each $k\in S$, so $\gcd(p-1,k)=k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Joint-closure theorem]\label{thm:joint}
Let $S$ be a finite set of positive integers and $q$ a prime. Then
\[
  \Big\{\,p \text{ prime}: p > q,\ \Prof_S(pq) = \big(k \mapsto k\gcd(q-1,k) \text{ on } S\big)\Big\}
\]
is infinite. Consequently there are distinct primes $p \ne p'$, both $> q$, with
$\Prof_S(pq) = \Prof_S(p'q)$, and there is \textbf{no} function $F$ with $F(\Prof_S(pq)) = p$ for all
primes $p>q$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $p \ne q$ saturate $S$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:sat}). By Theorem~\ref{thm:trace}, for $k \in S$,
$R_k(pq) = \gcd(p-1,k)\gcd(q-1,k) = k\gcd(q-1,k)$, independent of $p$. Since saturating primes exist
above any bound, the set is infinite; two distinct members give a collision. If such an $F$ existed
then $p = F(\Prof_S(pq)) = F(\Prof_S(p'q)) = p'$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Information-theoretic, not computational]
The colliding inputs are \emph{equal}, not merely indistinguishable to an efficient observer. No
running time, nondeterminism, or profile-dependent advice can separate them; the barrier applies to
unbounded adversaries.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}[Where the profile lives]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:trace}, $R_k(pq) \le k\gcd(q-1,k)$ for $k>0$: the saturating value is the top of a
finite divisor lattice, so collisions are forced by pigeonhole even before Dirichlet is invoked.
Dirichlet makes the colliding class explicit and constructive.
\end{remark}

\begin{example}
Take $S = \{6,12,15,20,30,60\}$ (least common multiple $60$) and $q=7$. Both $61$ and $181$ are primes
congruent to $1$ modulo $60$, hence saturate $S$; so the distinct semiprimes $427 = 61\cdot 7$ and
$1267 = 181\cdot 7$ carry the identical profile
$(36, 72, 45, 40, 180, 360)$ on $(6,12,15,20,30,60)$.
\end{example}

\section{The exact cost of completeness}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:cancel}
If $a \le A$, $b \le B$, $A,B>0$ and $ab = AB$, then $a=A$ and $b=B$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Completeness criterion]\label{thm:complete}
For $N = pq$ with $p \ne q$ prime,
\[
  R_k(N) = \varphi(N) \iff (p-1)\mid k \text{ and } (q-1)\mid k \iff \lcm(p-1,q-1) \mid k.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$R_k(N) = \gcd(p-1,k)\gcd(q-1,k)$ with factors bounded by $p-1$ and $q-1$. If the product equals
$(p-1)(q-1)$, Lemma~\ref{lem:cancel} forces both to be maximal, i.e.\ $(p-1)\mid k$, $(q-1)\mid k$;
conversely those divisibilities maximise each gcd. The last step is the universal property of the
least common multiple.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Completeness criterion, general]\label{thm:completegen}
For squarefree $N = \prod_{r\in P}r$, $R_k(N) = \varphi(N)$ iff $(r-1)\mid k$ for all $r \in P$. For
odd $N \ne 0$, $R_k(N)=\varphi(N)$ iff $\varphi(p^{v_p(N)}) \mid k$ for every prime $p \mid N$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{lemma}[Totient--trace identity]\label{lem:id}
For $p,q \ge 1$: $(p-1)(q-1) + (p+q) = pq+1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{definition}[Closed-form trace extractor]
For integers $N, s$ put $\Phi(N,s) = \tfrac12\big(s - \sqrt{s^2-4N}\big)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Trace inversion]\label{thm:inv}
If $p \le q$ then $\Phi(pq, p+q) = p$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $q = p+d$, $d \ge 0$. Then $s = 2p+d$ and $s^2 - 4pq = (2p+d)^2 - 4p(p+d) = d^2$, so
$\Phi = (2p+d-d)/2 = p$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[A complete witness yields the factorisation]\label{thm:cw}
Let $N = pq$ with $q \le p$ and suppose $R_k(N) = \varphi(N)$. Then
$\Phi\big(N,\, N+1-R_k(N)\big) = q$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:id}, $N+1-\varphi(N) = p+q$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:inv}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Crude aggregation cost]\label{thm:crude}
If $k>0$ and $R_k(pq) = \varphi(N)$ then $\varphi(N) \le k^2$, i.e.\ $k \ge \sqrt{\varphi(N)} \approx
\sqrt N$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Corollary~\ref{cor:budget}, $R_k(N) \mid k^2$ and $k^2>0$, so $R_k(N) \le k^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharp aggregation cost]\label{thm:sharp}
For $N = pq$,
\[
  \min\{k>0 : R_k(N)=\varphi(N)\} \;=\; \lcm(p-1,q-1) \;=\; \frac{\varphi(N)}{\gcd(p-1,q-1)},
\]
the complete exponents being exactly the positive multiples of this value; consequently
$\varphi(N) \le \gcd(p-1,q-1)\,k$ for every positive complete $k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:complete} the complete exponents are the multiples of $L=\lcm(p-1,q-1)>0$; a
positive multiple of $L$ is at least $L$, and $L$ is itself complete. Finally
$\gcd(p-1,q-1)\cdot L = (p-1)(q-1) = \varphi(N)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Aggregation depth of a general modulus]\label{thm:depth}
For squarefree $N = \prod_{r\in P}r$ the least positive complete exponent is $\lcm_{r\in P}(r-1)$; for
every odd $N \ne 0$ it is the Carmichael exponent
$\lambda(N) = \lcm_{p \mid N}\varphi(p^{v_p(N)})$, the exponent of $(\Z/N)^\times$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:completegen} the complete exponents are exactly the common multiples of the local
totients; the least positive such is their least common multiple, positive since each local totient
is.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Aggregation dichotomy]\label{thm:dich}
For $N = pq$ with $q \le p$: \emph{(i)} $\Phi\big(N, N+1-R_{\lcm(p-1,q-1)}(N)\big) = q$, and
\emph{(ii)} every positive $k$ with $R_k(N)=\varphi(N)$ satisfies $k \ge \sqrt{\varphi(N)}$, indeed
$k \ge \varphi(N)/\gcd(p-1,q-1)$.
\end{theorem}

For cryptographically generated primes one typically has $\gcd(p-1,q-1)=2$, so the least complete
exponent is $\varphi(N)/2 \approx N/2$: linear in $N$, exponential in $\log N$. Completeness and
efficiency are mutually exclusive within this channel.

\section{Localising the quantum advantage}

Shor's algorithm computes the multiplicative order $\ord_N(a)$ of a random unit $a$. That order is not
a quantity outside the classification: $\ord_N(a) \mid k$ iff $a$ is counted by $R_k(N)$, so the
family $\{R_k(N)\}_k$ is exactly the cumulative distribution of the order statistic, computed in
closed form by Theorem~\ref{thm:trace}. Order finding therefore computes a \emph{classified}
coordinate; what it does that classical methods cannot is \emph{locate} one cheaply.

\begin{theorem}[Residue-witness sufficiency]\label{thm:res}
Let $p \ne q$ be distinct odd primes, $N = pq$. There exists an integer $a$ with
\[
  N \mid a^2-1, \qquad N \nmid a-1, \qquad N \nmid a+1,
\]
and for any such $a$, $\gcd(a-1,N) \in \{p,q\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Existence is constructive: by Fact~\ref{fact:crt} let $a$ correspond to
$(1,-1) \in (\Z/p)^\times \times (\Z/q)^\times$; then $a^2 \leftrightarrow (1,1)$, so $N \mid a^2-1$,
while $a \equiv 1 \pmod N$ would force its $q$-component to be $1 \ne -1$ and $a \equiv -1 \pmod N$
would force its $p$-component to be $-1 \ne 1$ (both using that $p,q$ are odd). For sufficiency: from
$pq \mid (a-1)(a+1)$ each of $p,q$ divides $a-1$ or $a+1$; they cannot both divide the same one (else
$N \mid a \mp 1$), so exactly one divides $a-1$ and $\gcd(a-1,N)$ is that prime.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The witness is not rare]\label{thm:rare}
For $N = pq$ with distinct odd primes there are exactly $R_2(N)=4$ square roots of unity; two are
$\pm1$ and each of the other two splits $N$. For odd squarefree $N$ the count is $2^{\omega(N)}$, of
which two are trivial.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Corollaries~\ref{cor:four} and \ref{cor:count}, together with Theorem~\ref{thm:res}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Localisation]
The quantum speedup is attributable to the \emph{aggregation} step, not the classification: the
coordinate read is fully classified and its population known exactly
(Theorems~\ref{thm:trace},~\ref{thm:rare}); the post-processing to a factor is unconditional and
constant-time (Theorem~\ref{thm:res}); yet classically the coordinate is unreachable by any finite
aggregation (Theorem~\ref{thm:joint}) and reachable only at exponent depth $\lambda(N)$
(Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp}).
\end{corollary}

\section{Multiplicative walks are sterile}

\begin{definition}[Smooth-step walk]
For a modulus $N$, seed $x$ and step $s$, set $w_0 = x \bmod N$ and $w_{t+1} = w_t s \bmod N$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}
$w_t = x s^t \bmod N$ for all $t \ge 0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Sterility]\label{thm:sterile}
If $\gcd(x,N)=\gcd(s,N)=1$ then $\gcd(w_t,N)=1$ for every $t$; no walk value ever yields a nontrivial
divisor of $N$ through the gcd channel.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Induction on $t$: units are closed under multiplication and reduction modulo $N$ preserves coprimality
to $N$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The orbit is a coset]
If $s^r \equiv 1 \pmod N$ then $w_{t+r} = w_t$ for all $t$. In particular a unit step of
multiplicative order $r$ gives a walk of period dividing $r$, visiting at most $r$ distinct values.
\end{theorem}

The walk therefore carries no randomness beyond the cyclic subgroup $\langle s\rangle$; its only
exploitable resource is the smoothness of the visited values, which is exactly what sieve methods
already use and which yields subexponential --- never polynomial --- running time. Note the contrast
with Pollard's $\rho$, whose additive-quadratic iteration can leave the unit group, which is precisely
why it can emit a nontrivial gcd.

\section{Pricing an external hint: the scope of the framework}

\begin{theorem}[Hint amplification]\label{thm:hint}
Let $N = pq$ with $p \le q$ and let the hint $s = p+q$ be given. Then $\Phi(N,s) = p$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Hint completeness]
If $pq = p'q'$, $p+q = p'+q'$, $p \le q$, $p' \le q'$, then $p=p'$ and $q=q'$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$p = \Phi(pq,p+q) = \Phi(p'q',p'+q') = p'$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:inv}; then $q = (p+q)-p = q'$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Scope restatement]
Fix a prime $q$ and a finite set $S$ of positive exponents. Then simultaneously: \emph{(i)} there is
no $F$ with $F(\Prof_S(pq)) = p$ for all primes $p>q$; and \emph{(ii)} there is an explicit
$G(N,s) = \Phi(N,s)$ with $G(qp,\,q+p) = q$ for all primes $p>q$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{example}
$N = 8051$, $s = 180$: $s^2-4N = 196 = 14^2$ and $\Phi(8051,180) = (180-14)/2 = 83$; indeed
$8051 = 83\cdot 97$.
\end{example}

The framework is therefore about \emph{extraction from $N$ alone}. Hint amplification --- of which the
Coppersmith small-root methods are the practically important instance --- is a distinct and provably
powerful resource, and must be priced separately by any hardness claim.

\section{Synthesis}

\begin{theorem}[Exhaustion of the classical hint-free surface]
Let $p \ne q$ be distinct odd primes with $q<p$, $N = pq$, $S$ any finite set of positive exponents,
and $x,s$ units modulo $N$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{(Aggregation barrier.)} No function of $\Prof_S$ returns a prime factor.
\item \emph{(Walk sterility.)} $\gcd(w_t,N)=1$ for all $t$.
\item \emph{(Completeness at exponential depth.)}
  $\Phi\big(N, N+1-R_{\lcm(p-1,q-1)}(N)\big)=q$, while every positive complete exponent $k$ satisfies
  $k \ge \sqrt{\varphi(N)}$, indeed $k \ge \varphi(N)/\gcd(p-1,q-1)$.
\item \emph{(Residue sufficiency.)} There exists $a$ with $N \mid a^2-1$, $N \nmid a\pm1$ and
  $\gcd(a-1,N)\in\{p,q\}$; and exactly four square roots of unity exist modulo $N$.
\item \emph{(Hint amplification.)} $\Phi(N,p+q)=q$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
(1) Theorem~\ref{thm:joint}; (2) Theorem~\ref{thm:sterile}; (3) Theorems~\ref{thm:cw},
\ref{thm:crude}, \ref{thm:sharp}; (4) Theorems~\ref{thm:res}, \ref{thm:rare}; (5)
Theorem~\ref{thm:hint}.
\end{proof}

Items (1) and (3) are the two halves of the aggregation barrier --- impossibility below the threshold,
possibility only at exponential depth. Item (4) is the coordinate the quantum channel reads in one
shot; item (5) marks the boundary of the framework's scope.

\subsection{Numerical corroboration}

Brute-force counts of $\{x \in (\Z/N)^\times : x^k=1\}$ against the prediction
$\gcd(k,p-1)\gcd(k,q-1)$:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lcccccccc}
\toprule
$N$ & $k=1$ & $2$ & $3$ & $4$ & $5$ & $6$ & $7$ & $8$\\
\midrule
$15=3\cdot5$ & 1 & 4 & 1 & 8 & 1 & 4 & 1 & 8\\
$21=3\cdot7$ & 1 & 4 & 3 & 4 & 1 & 12 & 1 & 4\\
$35=5\cdot7$ & 1 & 4 & 3 & 8 & 1 & 12 & 1 & 8\\
