\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Saturating primes for $S=\{6,12,15,20,30,60\}$ (primes $\equiv 1 \bmod 60$): $61,181,241,421,541,601,
661,\dots$, all with identical joint profiles. The smooth-step walk $x \mapsto 2x \bmod 8051$ from
seed $3$ visits $3,6,12,24,48,\dots$, each coprime to $8051 = 83\cdot 97$. Hint amplification on
$8051$: $\Phi(8051,180)=83$.

\section{Discussion and future work}

\paragraph{What is and is not proved.} The results are \emph{channel-restricted}: an algorithm whose
only access to $N$ is through free witnesses cannot factor, and the family becomes informative only at
exponent depth $\lambda(N)$. They do not prove classical hardness of factoring --- lattice, sieve, and
elliptic-curve channels lie outside the model. Making the aggregation cost unavoidable for arbitrary
classical algorithms is precisely the open problem the framework isolates.

\begin{conjecture}[The 2-adic case]
For $N = 2^e M$ with $M$ odd and $e \ge 3$,
$R_k(N) = \gcd(2,k)\gcd(2^{e-2},k)\prod_{p\mid M}\gcd(\varphi(p^{v_p}),k)$, with least complete
exponent $\lcm(2^{e-2},\lambda(M))$ --- strictly smaller than $\varphi(N)$, so the even part shortens
the aggregation depth by a bounded factor, never enough to make it polynomial. The missing ingredient
is a root count in $\Z/2 \times \Z/2^{e-2}$, supplied by multiplicativity of the root count over
direct products.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Sparse infinite exponent sets]
For every infinite $S \subseteq \Z_{>0}$ with $\#(S\cap[1,X]) = O(X^{1-\varepsilon})$ and every prime
$q$, there are distinct primes $p \ne p'$ with $R_k(pq) = R_k(p'q)$ for all $k \in S\cap[1,N]$. The
Dirichlet argument needs only primes $\equiv 1$ modulo $\lcm(S\cap[1,X])$, which for sparse $S$ remains
small enough under standard prime-gap heuristics: sparsity, not finiteness, is the operative
hypothesis.
\end{conjecture}

\paragraph{Further questions.} Can the exponent-depth lower bound be promoted to a generic-group model
statement for algorithms with black-box access to arithmetic modulo $N$? And can \emph{any} efficiently
samplable classical stochastic process on $\Z/N$ have provably useful randomness beyond the birthday
floor of its orbit? All known candidates reduce either to smoothness (subexponential) or to order
finding (currently quantum).

\paragraph{Practical reading.} Exponent-probing attacks on RSA moduli are bounded by the Carmichael
exponent, and no combination of them improves matters. Guard $\varphi(N)$ and $\lambda(N)$ as you guard
$p$: either one, or the sum $p+q$, converts to the factorisation in constant time. And do not confuse
hint-free hardness with hardness under partial key exposure.

\section{Conclusion}

The free-witness family is completely classified: for every odd modulus,
$R_k(N) = \prod_{p\mid N}\gcd(\varphi(p^{v_p(N)}),k)$. From that single formula follow an
information-theoretic impossibility for finite aggregation, an exact aggregation depth equal to the
Carmichael exponent, the sterility of multiplicative walks, the exact population of useful residue
witnesses, and the closed-form amplification of the additive hint. Together these delimit a classical
attack surface, price it exactly, and localise the one known way through it: not a failure of the
classification, but a shortcut past its cost.

